\description{
Finds the directions in parameters and initial values along which
the observations of a model do not change: the nullspace of the observability
matrix over finite fields, with exact closed forms and a level that says whether
each one is proven. The model \code{f}, the observables \code{g} and an optional \code{trafo} are
equations in any form \code{\link[=as.eqnvec]{as.eqnvec()}} accepts. The computation runs in the Python
package \code{symident} through \code{reticulate}, installed from PyPI on first use.

Right-hand sides, observables and initial values may contain \code{exp()},
\code{exp10()}, \code{b^x}, hyperbolic and trigonometric functions, free exponents \code{x^n},
and \code{log()}, fractional powers and \code{abs()} of \code{positive} coordinates. \code{time} is
the model time.
}
\details{
The analysis starts at the earliest event; later events split the time
line into segments. Directions are reported in the original coordinates.

Every environment variable \verb{SYMIDENT_<NAME>} set in R, also with
\code{\link[=Sys.setenv]{Sys.setenv()}} during the session, is passed to symident for the call.
The environment variables a user may need
are \code{SYMIDENT_MSOLVE}, the path of the msolve executable (unset by
default, msolve is then searched in the symident cache and on the
\code{PATH}), and \code{SYMIDENT_CACHE}, the directory of that cache (by default
\verb{~/.cache/symident} on Linux). The other switches are diagnostic, see the
symident documentation at \url{https://dmodverse.github.io/symident/}.
}
